---
import { formatMs, runFor, toolOnlyMs } from "../../../lib/benchmarks";
import Speed from "../speed.astro";

// Bun against the two native engines; ESLint is compared on /providers. The
// tools-only times (lib/benchmarks) show where a check's time goes.
const large = runFor(420);
---

<Speed
  highlight="bun"
  id="bn-speed"
  stats={[
    {
      label: `For bun lint on the 420 files, on its own. Oxlint took ${Math.round(toolOnlyMs.oxlint)} ms`,
      unit: "ms",
      value: String(Math.round(toolOnlyMs.bunLint)),
    },
    {
      label: `For bun fmt --check on the same files. Oxfmt took ${Math.round(toolOnlyMs.oxfmt)} ms`,
      unit: "ms",
      value: String(Math.round(toolOnlyMs.bunFmt)),
    },
    {
      label: "To start Node and Ultracite, which every engine’s check pays first",
      unit: "ms",
      value: String(Math.round(toolOnlyMs.cliStartup)),
    },
  ]}
  title="Most of a check is starting Node"
  toolchains={["bun", "oxlint", "biome"]}
>
  On Bun, <code class="font-mono text-base">ultracite check</code> runs{" "}
  <code class="whitespace-nowrap font-mono text-base">bun fmt --check</code> and then{" "}
  <code class="font-mono text-base">bun lint</code>. Both are quicker than
  Oxfmt and Oxlint on the same files, but they’re the smaller part of the{" "}
  {formatMs(large.ms.bun)} a 420-file check takes. Most of the rest is
  Node and the CLI starting up, which every engine pays.
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    The figures under the chart time each tool on its own, in the same session.{" "}
    <a class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-foreground" href="/providers"
      >How ESLint compares</a
    >.
  </Fragment>
</Speed>
